WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Google
Cloud makes it easy for teams

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.560
to get the cloud resources
they need to build and host

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.039
applications, but there are
so many tools and services

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.440
that controlling costs and
managing your organization

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.120
can get tricky if
not set up properly.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:13.680
Don't worry.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.880
We're here to help.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.028
"Beyond Your Bill" is a
video series dedicated

00:00:17.028 --> 00:00:18.570
to helping you better
understand what

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.330
you're spending on the
cloud and best practices

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.160
for managing your
cloud environment.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.660
On this episode,
we'll take a look

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.820
at how to organize
your GCP resources.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.152
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.342 --> 00:00:35.800
Questions about
resource management

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.380
are top of mind for
many GCP administrators.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.620
In fact, the most common Google
Cloud billing support requests

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.510
focus on access
issues and confusion

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.670
around tracking
payment for services.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.770
To help you avoid having
these same issues,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.360
we'll go over some
best practices

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.460
and answer some common
questions such as,

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.080
how should I
structure my resources

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.410
for effective cost management,
and what's the ideal folder

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.750
structure for my organization?

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.510
First, let's start with
what we mean by a resource,

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.140
and then we'll cover how to best
organize them and grant access.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.750
Resources are defined as
any Google Cloud Platform

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.240
service such as Compute
Engine and BigQuery,

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.340
and they can exist
in a hierarchy.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.280
When we say resource hierarchy,
we're referring to the way

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.650
that you can organize your
Google Cloud environment

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.470
and how that maps to your
organization's structure,

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.750
letting you manage access
and permission for groups

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.460
of related resources.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.897
As you plan and set up
your resource hierarchy,

00:01:26.897 --> 00:01:28.480
make sure to consider
the requirements

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.890
for your organization.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.710
You can always change the
structure in the GCP console,

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.650
so start with the simplest
structure that meets your needs

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.440
and make changes as required.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.940
Here's a diagram of what the
different parts of the resource

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.290
hierarchy look like.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.943
Let's start from the top
and work our way down.

00:01:43.943 --> 00:01:45.610
Everything you'll
manage in Google Cloud

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.830
is under your domain
and organization.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:49.390
They're kind of
like an umbrella.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.150
If you're an individual user
with a G Suite or a Cloud

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.037
Identity account, you may
not have an organization,

00:01:55.037 --> 00:01:57.370
which means that some features
won't be available to you

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:58.690
until you create one.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.770
If you're getting started
with an organization,

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.240
or if you're migrating existing
projects to a new organization,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.918
the organization setup wizard
may be a good place to begin.

00:02:07.918 --> 00:02:10.210
The wizard guides you through
recommendations and steps

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.580
for setting up
everything, so check out

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:14.290
the link for more information.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.470
The domain is handled through
G Suite or Cloud Identity

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.390
and helps you manage
user profiles,

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.450
while the organization is
managed through the GCP console

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.020
and lets you manage GCP
resources and access.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.700
With an organization,
projects belong to the org

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.950
rather than to the
user that created them.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.440
Org admins can see and
manage all projects

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.330
as well as set up
permissions and policies

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.150
that'll be inherited for
different groups of resources.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.910
This is especially helpful when
a team member creates a project

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.350
and then later
leaves the company.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.810
Each organization and domain
have separate permission

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:44.620
settings.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.860
Make sure to set up multiple
admins or use a group for each

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:50.320
to ensure a reasonable
level of redundancy.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.960
Billing accounts are under
the organization and track

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.582
any charges for
associated projects.

00:02:55.582 --> 00:02:57.040
A billing account
has its own roles

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.230
and permissions so
you can manage users

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.030
for billing related functions.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.580
Billing account users
can associate projects

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.820
and see spend, while billing
account admins can additionally

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.720
unlink projects, enable billing
export, and set budgets.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.850
In addition, only
billing account admins

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.220
can contact billing
support, so make

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.740
sure to have more than
one admin or use a group

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.960
and ensure your team
knows who they are.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.790
We recommend sticking
to a single billing

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.190
account per organization
and making sure

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.860
only your admins can create
new billing accounts.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.170
You can do that by removing
the billing account creator

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.150
role from your organization.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.435
In most cases, you'll only
need one billing account,

00:03:33.435 --> 00:03:34.810
and creating
multiple can make it

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.667
tricky to track down payments.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.500
If you need to deal
with multiple currencies

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.660
or follow certain
legal requirements,

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:42.790
you may have to create
multiple billing accounts,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.183
but don't forget to still have
redundancy for your admins.

00:03:46.183 --> 00:03:47.600
When setting up a
billing account,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.720
the admins should
enable BigQuery export

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.620
as soon as possible since the
export data isn't retroactive.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.840
This way, your usage data
will be tracked and ready

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.165
for analysis using
a variety of tools.

00:03:58.165 --> 00:04:00.040
We'll talk more about
exporting and analyzing

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.530
billing data in later videos.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.720
The payments profile is
a Google level resource

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.610
that sits outside
of Google Cloud

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.990
and is used for paying
for all Google services,

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.050
such as Google Ads
or Chrome licenses.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.300
It's possible to have the
same admins for your billing

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.040
account and your
payment profile,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.220
but make sure that you still
set up multiple administrators

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.510
or use groups.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.940
In the payment profile
settings, review the preferences

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.975
for notifications and
statements as well as

00:04:23.975 --> 00:04:25.600
ensuring that you
have invoice delivery

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.060
set up for email
and paper invoices.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.637
Having multiple admins, payment
methods, and notification

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:33.220
methods will make sure that you
get the right information when

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.100
you need it and avoid
any potential issues.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.260
Projects are used for
grouping any resources

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.810
like Google Kubernetes
Engine or Cloud Storage.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.700
A project can exist
under a folder,

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.950
so it can be grouped logically
to match your company's

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:47.710
structure as well as inherit
any permissions from folders

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.590
above it or from the
organization at the top.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.450
Projects can also
have labels across

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:55.480
your entire organization, which
you can use to horizontally

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:57.250
group projects and services.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.960
Folders and projects
can have permissions

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.720
that let you control who can
create, edit, or just view

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.350
resources inside of them.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.170
For example, you could
set up certain users

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.670
as project creators
so that they can

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:09.100
create new projects,
or just project viewers

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.780
so that they can see
what's being used

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.540
and view the costs for
an individual project.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.600
The permissions and structure
are flexible and changeable,

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.240
so you can organize your
hierarchy to meet your needs.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.990
Here's an example of matching
an organization's structure.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.970
In this image, the
first level of folders

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:28.660
represents departments, the next
level teams, and then products

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.277
with labels for environments
like production.

00:05:31.277 --> 00:05:32.860
Another example might
be using folders

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.740
for environments and
labels for teams,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:38.160
or folders for lines of business
and labels for applications.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:39.840
A project can have
multiple labels,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.410
but it can only directly
exist under a single folder.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.300
For projects,
labels, and folders,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.100
ensure that you use
naming conventions

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.940
that are familiar to your team.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.450
These names are referenced in
the console, billing reports,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.310
and for resource organization.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:56.280
It's imperative that clear
and well-understood naming is

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.258
used across your organization.

00:05:58.258 --> 00:06:00.300
Of course, the specifics
will depend on your team

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.762
and your governance.

00:06:01.762 --> 00:06:03.720
Ultimately, the structure
and naming convention

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.210
are meant to follow whatever
works best for your org.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.610
Taking the time upfront to
plan your resource structure

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.080
for what's right
for your business

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.020
can save you a lot of confusion
and hassle down the road.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.910
With that overview of
the resource hierarchy,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.100
the next step is to talk
more about billing accounts

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.312
and the payments profile.

00:06:18.312 --> 00:06:19.770
I definitely
recommend checking out

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:22.560
the links for a deeper dive
and for more best practices

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.290
around resource organization.

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:26.400
In the next video, we'll
set up a billing account,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.280
go over permissions, and
discuss more best practices.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

